---
import Marketing from '../layouts/Marketing.astro';
import { site } from '../data/project';

const updated = '27 October 2026';
---

<Marketing title="Privacy policy · Outflare" description="What Outflare collects on outflare.ai and in the hosted app at app.outflare.ai, how Google user data is accessed, used, stored, shared and deleted, and how to exercise your rights.">
	<section class="relative border-b">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 sm:py-20">
			<p class="eyebrow">Legal</p>
			<h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tighter text-balance sm:text-5xl">Privacy policy</h1>
			<p class="sub">Last updated {updated}.</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-14 sm:px-6">
		<div class="legal">
			<h2>Who we are</h2>
			<p>
				Outflare is open-source marketing software, used by people and their AI agents, made by the Outflare team. This policy covers the website at outflare.ai and the hosted app at app.outflare.ai (together, "Outflare", "we", "us"). Questions about it go to
				<a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				If you run Outflare on your own server, your data stays on that server and we never see it. The only thing a self-hosted server sends us is the{' '}
				<a href="/docs/self-hosting/telemetry/">anonymous daily report</a>, which contains no personal data and can be turned off.
			</p>

			<h2>Information we collect</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Account information:</strong> your name, email address, a hashed password or a passkey, and the workspaces you belong to.</li>
				<li><strong>Information you add:</strong> mailboxes you connect (with their passwords or OAuth tokens, encrypted at rest), domains, leads and their contact details, sequences, the emails you send and the replies you receive, lists, projects and settings.</li>
				<li><strong>Google user data:</strong> only when you choose to connect a Google account, as described in full in the next section.</li>
				<li><strong>Data from other tools you connect:</strong> for example analytics, calendars, CRMs or mailbox suppliers, read with the access you grant.</li>
				<li><strong>Usage and device data:</strong> sign-in times, IP address, browser, the pages of the app you use and the actions you take, and a daily count of what each workspace used.</li>
				<li><strong>Messages to us:</strong> what you send to {site.email}.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2 id="google-user-data">Google user data</h2>
			<p>This section applies to the Outflare app at app.outflare.ai, which appears as "Outflare" on Google's sign-in and consent screens. It explains what Google user data Outflare accesses, how it is used, who it is shared with, how it is protected, and how long it is kept and how it is deleted.</p>
			<p>Outflare only accesses Google user data after you click a "Continue with Google" or "Connect" button and approve Google's consent screen. Each feature asks separately for only the access it needs, and you can disconnect it at any time.</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 font-semibold text-foreground">Data accessed</h3>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Sign in with Google</strong> (<code>openid</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>profile</code>): your Google account ID, email address, name and profile picture.</li>
				<li><strong>Google Analytics</strong> (<code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/analytics.readonly</code>): the list of Analytics accounts and properties you can access, and read-only report data for the property you choose, such as visitors, sessions, pages, traffic sources and events. Outflare cannot change anything in your Analytics account.</li>
				<li><strong>Google Search Console</strong> (<code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/webmasters.readonly</code>): the list of sites you have verified, and read-only search performance for the site you choose, such as queries, pages, clicks, impressions and average position. Outflare cannot change anything in Search Console.</li>
				<li><strong>Gmail mailboxes</strong> (<code>https://mail.google.com/</code>), only if you connect a Gmail mailbox: Outflare sends the emails you write and schedule from that mailbox and reads new mail arriving in its inbox from the day before you connect it, so replies appear in your Outflare inbox. Older mail is not imported and mail is never deleted.</li>
				<li><strong>Google Calendar</strong> (<code>calendar.events.readonly</code>), only if you connect a calendar: event titles, times, status and attendee email addresses, read only, so meetings with your contacts can be counted in your reports.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>Outflare does not access your Google contacts, Drive files or any other Google data.</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 font-semibold text-foreground">How Google user data is used</h3>
			<ul>
				<li>Sign-in data is used to create your Outflare account, sign you in and show your name and picture in the app.</li>
				<li>Google Analytics and Search Console data is used to show your website's traffic and search performance in your workspace's reports and charts, to compare periods and sources, and to answer your questions about it when you ask an AI assistant connected to your own workspace.</li>
				<li>Gmail access is used only to send the emails you write and to show replies in your Outflare inbox.</li>
				<li>Calendar data is used only to count meetings in your reports.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Google user data is used only to provide and improve these features for you. It is not used for advertising, it is not sold, it is not used to build profiles for anyone else, and it is not used to develop, improve or train generalised or non-personalised AI or machine-learning models. Outflare's use and transfer to any other app of information received from Google APIs will adhere to the
				<a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>, including the Limited Use requirements.
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 font-semibold text-foreground">Sharing, transfer and disclosure of Google user data</h3>
			<p>Outflare does not sell, rent or share Google user data with advertisers, data brokers or other third parties. It is disclosed only:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>to the service providers that host and run Outflare for us under contract and only to provide the service: Railway (application hosting and database, United States) and Cloudflare (network and email delivery);</li>
				<li>to people and AI assistants you choose to give access to your workspace, such as teammates you invite;</li>
				<li>when required by law, or to protect the safety and security of our users and the service, with notice to you where the law allows.</li>
			</ul>

			<h3 class="pt-2 font-semibold text-foreground">Storage and data protection</h3>
			<ul>
				<li>Google user data is stored in our production database in the United States.</li>
				<li>OAuth access and refresh tokens are encrypted at rest with AES-256-GCM, using a key held only by the running service and never stored in the database or source code.</li>
				<li>All traffic between your browser, Outflare and Google is encrypted in transit with TLS.</li>
				<li>Every workspace's data is kept separate, and every request is checked against the workspace it belongs to.</li>
				<li>Access to production systems is limited to the people who run the service. People at Outflare do not read your Google data unless you ask us to for support, it is needed to investigate a security issue or abuse, or the law requires it.</li>
			</ul>

			<h3 class="pt-2 font-semibold text-foreground">Retention and deletion of Google user data</h3>
			<ul>
				<li>OAuth tokens are kept only while the connection is active. When you disconnect a Google tool in Outflare, or revoke access in your <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions">Google account permissions</a>, its tokens are deleted.</li>
				<li>Analytics, Search Console and Calendar report data is kept as history for your reports until you disconnect the tool or delete the workspace.</li>
				<li>Emails sent through a Gmail mailbox and replies read from it are kept in your workspace until you delete them, the mailbox or the workspace.</li>
				<li>When you delete a workspace or close your account, all of its data, including Google user data, is deleted within 30 days, and encrypted backups roll off within a further 30 days.</li>
				<li>You can ask us to delete your Google user data at any time by emailing <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>. We do it within 30 days.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>How we use information</h2>
			<p>We use the information above to provide the service you asked for: to sign you in, send and receive the email you set up, run your sequences, show your reports, keep the service secure and prevent abuse, understand how the product is used so we can improve it, and contact you about your account. We do not sell personal data.</p>

			<h2>Who we share it with</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Railway</strong> hosts the app and its database in the United States.</li>
				<li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts this website and our DNS, and sends account emails such as confirmations and password resets.</li>
				<li><strong>PostHog</strong> records how the app and this website are used (see below).</li>
				<li><strong>Google Analytics</strong> may measure visits to this website (see below).</li>
				<li><strong>Providers you connect</strong>, such as Google, Microsoft, a mailbox supplier or a data provider, receive what's needed for the action you take, under their own terms.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>We may also disclose information if the law requires it, or to protect people and the service from fraud or abuse.</p>

			<h2>Cookies and analytics</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>This website</strong> measures visits with PostHog in cookieless mode, and may use Google Analytics through Google Tag Manager with Google's consent mode (no analytics cookies in the EEA, UK and Switzerland), Cloudflare Web Analytics, which sets no cookies, and Outflare's own website tracking. Outflare's tracking keeps a random visitor id in an <code>of_aid</code> cookie for up to a year, shared with app.outflare.ai, except in Europe or when your browser sends Global Privacy Control, where it sets no cookie.</li>
				<li><strong>The app</strong> uses a session cookie to keep you signed in, and PostHog to understand which features are used. Text on the page, form inputs and element attributes are masked, and URLs are reduced to the page name, so the content of your workspace is not sent to PostHog. Outflare's own website tracking also counts the app's pages, with the same visitor id as on this website; it records each page's address and title, which can include your workspace's name, and keeps them in Outflare's own database.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>International transfers</h2>
			<p>We and our providers process data in the United States and other countries. Where the law requires, transfers are covered by the providers' standard contractual clauses.</p>

			<h2>Children</h2>
			<p>Outflare is a business tool and is not meant for anyone under 16. We do not knowingly collect their data.</p>

			<h2>Your rights</h2>
			<p>You can see and change most of your data in the app, export it through the API, and delete mailboxes, workspaces or your account. You can also ask us to access, correct, export or delete your personal data, or object to how we use it: email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> and we'll reply within 30 days. You may also complain to your local privacy regulator.</p>

			<h2>Security</h2>
			<p>Traffic is encrypted in transit, credentials and tokens are encrypted at rest, and access is limited to the people who run the service. To report a vulnerability, see our <a href="/docs/project/security/">security policy</a>.</p>

			<h2>Changes</h2>
			<p>If this policy changes in a way that matters, we'll update the date above and tell account holders by email before it takes effect.</p>

			<h2>Contact</h2>
			<p><a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a></p>
		</div>
	</div>
</Marketing>
